Source: kubeos-kernel
Section: kernel
Priority: optional
Maintainer: KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13)
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Rules-Requires-Root: no
Homepage: https://kubeos.in/

Package: linux-image-kubeos
Architecture: amd64
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, linux-image-6.12.117-kubeos (= 6.12.117-1kubeos1),
 linux-base, kmod, initramfs-tools | linux-initramfs-tool
Conflicts: linux-image-amd64
Description: KubeOS: the kernel flavour a KubeOS node boots
 Names the exact kernel a KubeOS node runs, and is what makes "which kernel does this
 KubeOS run?" answerable with one command:
 .
     dpkg-query -W linux-image-kubeos
 .
 The kernel itself is linux-image-6.12.117-kubeos, built by KubeOS from the upstream
 6.12.117 tree with CONFIG_LOCALVERSION="-kubeos" (so it appears as 6.12.117-kubeos in
 uname -r) and only reachable from repo.kubeos.in, exactly like kubeos-kubernetes pins
 Kubernetes: `apt upgrade` on a node can never move it to a kernel KubeOS has not vetted.
 .
 The package is named linux-image-kubeos, not kubeos-kernel, because live-build decides
 which kernel an ISO gets from --linux-packages and --linux-flavours: it installs
 "${--linux-packages}-${--linux-flavours}" and every later stage finds the kernel by
 globbing vmlinuz-*<flavour>. The flavour therefore has to be "kubeos", and
 --linux-packages "linux-image" turns that into the Debian-conventional name used here -
 the same shape as linux-image-amd64. auto/config therefore never changes when the kernel
 is rebuilt; only this package's dependency and version move.
 .
 Ship only one kernel on a node: this package conflicts with linux-image-amd64, because
 the ISO's boot entries and initramfs are built for this kernel and a second, Debian one
 would silently become the default at the next upgrade.
 .
 The dependencies above are not decoration. The KubeOS kernel deb is built with
 `make deb-pkg` and declares no Depends at all, so dpkg is free to configure it BEFORE
 initramfs-tools - and then nothing generates /boot/initrd.img-<version>. Measured on the
 2026-11-22 ISO build: the kernel was configured 116 log lines before initramfs-tools, the
 initramfs-tools trigger generated nothing, and live-build's binary_syslinux died with
 "cannot access 'binary/live/initrd.img-*'". Declaring them here, plus the postinst that runs
 update-initramfs, is what makes this package - not luck - produce a bootable kernel.
 .
 Secure Boot: the kernel is NOT signed. Debian's is, and an ISO carries shim, so on a
 machine with UEFI Secure Boot enabled this kernel is refused at boot. Secure Boot must be
 off on a KubeOS node (documented in docs/USER-GUIDE.md).
